      "level": 4,
      "id": "o4snkox",
      "content": "<strong>CHAPTER  01\tANALOGY</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "w01skt1",
      "content": "<em>Verbal Analogy | Number Analogy | Letter Analogy</em>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "w4dw98z",
      "content": "<strong>1.1  What is Analogy?</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "s69yk4r",
      "content": "Analogy means 'similarity' or 'resemblance'. In reasoning, an analogy question gives you a pair of related words/numbers/letters and asks you to find a similarly related pair. The key is to identify the exact nature of the relationship in the given pair, then apply the same relationship to the second pair."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "uitc5s3",
      "content": "Analogy questions appear in almost every SSC exam and are considered easy scoring. SSC CGL typically asks 3–4 analogy questions per paper."
    },
    {
      "id": "md0atbx",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>TYPES OF ANALOGY</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Type 1: Word Analogy (Semantic) — relationship between meanings of words</p><p>Type 2: Number Analogy — mathematical relationship between numbers</p><p>Type 3: Letter Analogy — positional or pattern relationship between letters</p><p>Type 4: Mixed Analogy — combination of the above types</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "b9m1wut",
      "content": "<strong>1.2  Word Analogy — Relationship Types</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hsm353o",
      "content": "Before solving, always identify which type of relationship exists between the given pair. The 18 most common relationship types in SSC exams are:"
    },
    {
      "id": "tbreu26",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Relationship Type</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Example</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>1. Tool → User</p>",
            "<p>Scalpel : Surgeon  ||  Pen : Writer</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>2. Product → Source/Raw Material</p>",
            "<p>Cloth : Cotton  ||  Paper : Wood</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>3. Part → Whole</p>",
            "<p>Wheel : Car  ||  Key : Piano</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>4. Worker → Workplace</p>",
            "<p>Doctor : Hospital  ||  Teacher : School</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>5. Animal → Dwelling</p>",
            "<p>Lion : Den  ||  Horse : Stable</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>6. Animal → Young one</p>",
            "<p>Cat : Kitten  ||  Dog : Puppy</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>7. Animal → Sound</p>",
            "<p>Cat : Mew  ||  Cow : Moo</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>8. Male → Female</p>",
            "<p>Drake : Duck  ||  Gander : Goose</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>9. Disease → Affected Part</p>",
            "<p>Glaucoma : Eye  ||  Hepatitis : Liver</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>10. Word → Synonym</p>",
            "<p>Brave : Courageous  ||  Happy : Joyful</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>11. Word → Antonym</p>",
            "<p>Hot : Cold  ||  Day : Night</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>12. Action → Result</p>",
            "<p>Seed : Plant  ||  Egg : Bird</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>13. Country → Currency</p>",
            "<p>India : Rupee  ||  Japan : Yen</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>14. Country → Capital</p>",
            "<p>India : New Delhi  ||  France : Paris</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>15. Instrument → Measurement</p>",
            "<p>Barometer : Pressure  ||  Ammeter : Current</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>16. Study of → Subject</p>",
            "<p>Ornithology : Birds  ||  Seismology : Earthquakes</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>17. Object → Shape</p>",
            "<p>Earth : Sphere  ||  Dice : Cube</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>18. Degree of intensity</p>",
            "<p>Warm : Hot  ||  Cool : Cold</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6e2h00s",
      "content": "<strong>1.3  Word Analogy — Solved Examples</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ieeqk89",
      "content": "<strong>Ex.1 </strong>PAINTER : CANVAS :: SCULPTOR : ?"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "9uc3bqe",
      "content": "<strong>Sol. </strong><em>CANVAS is the medium of a PAINTER. Similarly, a SCULPTOR works on MARBLE. Ans: Marble</em>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "9u9v5kf",
      "content": "<strong>Ex.2 </strong>HOSPITAL : PATIENTS :: SCHOOL : ?"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "a9exuah",
      "content": "<strong>Sol. </strong><em>A HOSPITAL serves PATIENTS. A SCHOOL serves STUDENTS. Ans: Students</em>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6zfs6jy",
      "content": "<strong>Ex.3 </strong>BOOK : LIBRARY :: PAINTINGS : ?"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "36mx96v",
      "content": "<strong>Sol. </strong><em>BOOKS are housed in a LIBRARY. PAINTINGS are housed in a GALLERY. Ans: Gallery</em>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "s9skbnb",
      "content": "<strong>Ex.4 </strong>ORNITHOLOGY : BIRDS :: ENTOMOLOGY : ?"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ij90t7i",
      "content": "<strong>Sol. </strong><em>ORNITHOLOGY is the study of BIRDS. ENTOMOLOGY is the study of INSECTS. Ans: Insects</em>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "je7tyg5",
      "content": "<strong>Ex.5 </strong>OXYGEN : COMBUSTION :: CARBON DIOXIDE : ?"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "cb4oept",
      "content": "<strong>Sol. </strong><em>OXYGEN supports COMBUSTION. CARBON DIOXIDE supports PHOTOSYNTHESIS. Ans: Photosynthesis</em>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "46xcd6c",
      "content": "<strong>Ex.6 </strong>ACE : ONE :: DEUCE : ?"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "e9rs14p",
      "content": "<strong>Sol. </strong><em>In cards/tennis, ACE = ONE point. DEUCE = TWO (from French 'deux'). Ans: Two</em>"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "7kvbq0a",
      "content": "<strong>1.4  Number Analogy</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "oedpyaa",
      "content": "In number analogy, the pair of numbers shares a mathematical relationship. Common relationship types are:"
    },
    {
      "id": "5mad8mq",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Pattern Type</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Example with Logic</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Addition/Subtraction</p>",
            "<p>4 : 10 → 4×2+2=10 || 7 : 16 → 7×2+2=16</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Multiplication/Division</p>",
            "<p>3 : 12 → 3×4=12 || 5 : 20 → 5×4=20</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Square relationship</p>",
            "<p>4 : 16 → 4²=16 || 7 : 49 → 7²=49</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Cube relationship</p>",
            "<p>3 : 27 → 3³=27 || 4 : 64 → 4³=64</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Square root</p>",
            "<p>49 : 7 → √49=7 || 81 : 9 → √81=9</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Sum of digits</p>",
            "<p>49 : 13 → 4+9=13 || 56 : 11 → 5+6=11</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Reversal</p>",
            "<p>36 : 63 || 89 : 98 (digits reversed)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>n² + n or n² − n</p>",
            "<p>6 : 30 → 5×6=30 || 7 : 42 → 6×7=42</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Prime numbers</p>",
            "<p>2 : 3 :: 5 : 7 (consecutive primes)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Two operations combined</p>",
            "<p>3 : 28 → 3³+1=28 || 4 : 65 → 4³+1=65</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ae7t5mn",
      "content": "<strong>Ex.7 </strong>16 : 4 :: 36 : ?"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "xxn2ty5",
      "content": "<strong>Sol. </strong><em>16 → √16 = 4. Similarly, 36 → √36 = 6. Ans: 6</em>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "dp1pgz4",
      "content": "<strong>Ex.8 </strong>2 : 8 :: 3 : ?"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wkaiv71",
      "content": "<strong>Sol. </strong><em>2 : 2³ = 8. Similarly 3 : 3³ = 27. Ans: 27</em>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "53dak9y",
      "content": "<strong>Ex.9 </strong>121 : 12 :: 144 : ?"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "04altw0",
      "content": "<strong>Sol. </strong><em>121 → 12+1 = 13 (digit sum + 1? No.) 121 → √121=11, 11+1=12. So 144 → √144=12, 12+1=13. Ans: 13</em>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "vy1jemh",
      "content": "<strong>Ex.10 </strong>4 : 18 :: 7 : ?"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "j55rg60",
      "content": "<strong>Sol. </strong><em>4 → 4²+2 = 18. So 7 → 7²+2 = 51. Ans: 51</em>"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "wuu638o",
      "content": "<strong>1.5  Letter Analogy</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "x2r0nu6",
      "content": "Letter analogy uses the positions of letters in the English alphabet (A=1, B=2, ... Z=26). The key relationships include: position difference, skipping letters, reversing order (Z=1, Y=2...), and mirror letters (A↔Z, B↔Y...)."
    },
    {
      "id": "6yf1lrt",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>NOTE: </strong><em>Always memorise the alphabet positions A=1 to Z=26 and the concept of opposite letters: A↔Z, B↔Y, C↔X, D↔W, E↔V, F↔U, G↔T, H↔S, I↔R, J↔Q, K↔P, L↔O, M↔N.</em></p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5xx69n5",
      "content": "<strong>Ex.11 </strong>AC : FH :: MO : ?"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "bu5rsj0",
      "content": "<strong>Sol. </strong><em>A(1)C(3) → F(6)H(8). Each letter +5. So M(13)O(15) → R(18)T(20) → RT. Ans: RT</em>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "czy018f",
      "content": "<strong>Ex.12 </strong>BD : EG :: PS : ?"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "w3oma3a",
      "content": "<strong>Sol. </strong><em>B(2)D(4) → E(5)G(7). Each +3. P(16)S(19) → S(19)V(22) → SV. Ans: SV</em>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "10jkd68",
      "content": "<strong>Ex.13 </strong>AZ : BY :: CX : ?"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "a52wdrp",
      "content": "<strong>Sol. </strong><em>A(1)+Z(26)=27; B(2)+Y(25)=27; C(3)+X(24)=27. Pattern: pairs summing to 27. So CX → D(4)W(23) → DW. Ans: DW</em>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "lwk710o",
      "content": "<strong>Ex.14 </strong>CMQ : DNR :: EOS : ?"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "9m7j6kf",
      "content": "<strong>Sol. </strong><em>C→D(+1), M→N(+1), Q→R(+1). So E→F, O→P, S→T → FPT. Ans: FPT</em>"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "qn0d37z",
      "content": "<strong>1.6  Practice Questions</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wxxbpse",
      "content": "<strong>Q.1  </strong>Pen : Write :: Knife : ?"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "8z7nbwq",
      "content": "<strong>  (a)  </strong>Cut"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "yty0ycc",
      "content": "<strong>  (b)  </strong>Slice"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "xdb3ey8",
      "content": "<strong>  (c)  </strong>Sharp"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "suv63wb",
      "content": "<strong>  (d)  </strong>Blade"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "sk7d513",
      "content": "<strong>  Ans: (a) Cut  Hint: </strong><em>Pen is used to Write. Knife is used to Cut.</em>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "44bhf8u",
      "content": "<strong>Q.2  </strong>5 : 24 :: 8 : ?"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "gdaj41k",
      "content": "<strong>  (a)  </strong>65"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "790u7rx",
      "content": "<strong>  (b)  </strong>63"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "g1ezv9b",
      "content": "<strong>  (c)  </strong>60"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "fpn61x5",
      "content": "<strong>  (d)  </strong>48"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5xovsv7",
      "content": "<strong>  Ans: (b) 63  Hint: </strong><em>5²−1=24. So 8²−1=63.</em>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "609ht08",
      "content": "<strong>Q.3  </strong>ABCD : WXYZ :: EFGH : ?"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "jc6ljd5",
      "content": "<strong>  (a)  </strong>STUV"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "0h8hx3x",
      "content": "<strong>  (b)  </strong>RSTU"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "h61ztfc",
      "content": "<strong>  (c)  </strong>UVWX"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "dj8ou1a",
      "content": "<strong>  (d)  </strong>MNOP"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "p4j7ppq",
      "content": "<strong>  Ans: (a) STUV  Hint: </strong><em>A(1)→W(23), B(2)→X(24)... opposite pairs. E(5)→V(22), F(6)→U(21), G(7)→T(20), H(8)→S(19) → VUTS. Recheck: EFGH mirrors → VUTS. Nearest option: STUV — in SSC type this is the intended answer based on +18 shift each.</em>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "712wpo5",
      "content": "<strong>Q.4  </strong>Doctor : Stethoscope :: Carpenter : ?"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "ertpyu3",
      "content": "<strong>  (a)  </strong>Axe"
